<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abeokuta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abeokuta is a city in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> founded in the 1830s by Egba <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> refugees. Its foundation was one consequence of the wars and population movements that followed the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. It developed into an important Yoruba town and is noted in the Oyo Empire's profile as part of the region's post-imperial landscape.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abeokuta.jpg" alt="Abeokuta" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diplenty multimedia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_cultural_center_abeokuta_side_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Abeokuta lies on both banks of the Ogun River, in fertile wooded savanna broken by masses of grey granite, 77 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> by railway. The city stands below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olumo-rock">Olumo Rock</a>, which has caves and shrines and carries spiritual significance. Its name already refers to several rock formations in the neighbourhood. Mud walls 29 kilometres long once surrounded it, and remnants still stand. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">Palm oil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, natural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, yams, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and shea butter were the chief articles of trade, and the city exports cocoa, palm products and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kola-nut">kola nuts</a>. Rice and cotton were introduced by missionaries in the 1850s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Accounts of the foundation differ in detail. All agree that Abeokuta was founded by Egba people around 1830 on and around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olumo-rock">Olumo Rock</a>, at what had been a small farming village, as a refuge for displaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> during the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> and the wars that followed, and from slave hunters sent by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>. One version says it replaced Orile Egba, a confederacy of towns said to date from about the 13th century. Another says the Egba withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a> after a chief named Lamodi shot an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> chief named Ege, and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sodeke">Sodeke</a> as their leader. Other Egba and many Owu refugees soon joined them, but the groups did not fuse: Abeokuta was a federation of communities within a town wall.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a> grew hostile because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palm-oil">palm oil</a> trade. In the Battle of Abeokuta in 1851 the Egba defeated King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghezo">Gezo</a>, and they beat back Dahomey again in 1864. In 1867 they expelled missionaries and traders after friction with the British at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>. In 1893, during the Yoruba Civil Wars, the alake of the Egba signed an alliance with the British governor, Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-carter">Gilbert Carter</a>, which formalised the Egba United Government. The Egba lands joined the colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> in 1914 with Abeokuta as provincial capital. The Adubi War of 1918 resisted taxation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, and the Abeokuta Women's Union led a revolt against unfair taxation in the 1940s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Abeokuta has been the capital of Ogun State since 1976. The railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, 77 kilometres long, was completed in 1899. Ake, the traditional residence of the alake, and Centenary Hall of 1930 stand in the Egba Alake's territory, and the Federal University of Agriculture, Abeokuta, was established in 1988. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> of 2006 counted 449,088 people in its two local government areas.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abeokuta” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Old Yoruba cities such as Old Oyo (Oyo-ile), Ijaye and Owu disappeared in those years, and newer ones, <b>Abeokuta</b>, New Oyo (Oyo Atiba) and Ibadan, rose to replace them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ibadan">Ibadan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They also suffered heavy defeats: Oyo's cavalry invaded repeatedly in the 1720s, and the Egba stronghold of <b>Abeokuta</b> repulsed Dahomean assaults in 1851 and 1864 with severe losses.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-dahomey">Kingdom of Dahomey · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Refugees and warriors from the Oyo heartland founded or enlarged new centres such as Ibadan, <b>Abeokuta</b> and Ijaye, which became the leading Yoruba powers of the nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In West Africa, meanwhile, Christianity and Islam spread rapidly among the Yoruba from the 19th century: missionaries arrived in <b>Abeokuta</b> in the 1840s, and the Yoruba Samuel Ajayi Crowther, a freed slave, became the first African Anglican bishop in 1864, while Islam spread from the north.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yoruba-religion">Yoruba religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Abeokuta</b> inflicted crushing defeats on them in the 19th century, and France defeated them in 1890 and 1892–1894.</blockquote><a href="/societies/dahomey">Social Hierarchy of the Kingdom of Dahomey</a></li><li><blockquote>Kuti's politics owed much to his mother, Funmilayo Ransome-Kuti, an anti-colonial activist who led the <b>Abeokuta</b> Market Women's Movement in the 1940s, and to a visit to the United States in 1969, where he met the Black power movement and Pan-Africanist writing.</blockquote><a href="/songs/zombie">Zombie</a></li></ul></section>
